; Exercise 09-receipt (chapter 9).
;
; A shopping list has one item per line: a quantity, a price in cents and
; a name, separated by single spaces:  "2 350 coffee"
;
; (receipt lines) returns the text of the receipt, one String:
;   one line per good item:  "2 x coffee = 7.00\n"
;   then the total:          "total 7.00\n"
;   then one line per bad line, in order:  "bad line: <the line>\n"
; Amounts are written in units with two decimals: 700 cents is "7.00".
; A line is bad when it does not have three parts, or when the quantity
; or the price is not an Integer. The name is everything after the price,
; so "1 99 ice cream" is a good line named "ice cream".
;
; A program would print (receipt (read-lines!)).
; Then run: tutorial/check.sh 09-receipt

